Why do most systems stop improving over time?
Answer:
Most systems stop improving because outcomes are not reviewed systematically. Feedback becomes anecdotal, failures are ignored or blamed, and success hides flaws.
Without structure, learning decays. Experts counter this by formalizing review, thresholds, and versioned changes.

